{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/6/codegen_prompts/2.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/6`\n- 题号：`2`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/6/2.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"2\">\n- `description`：识别网格中的箭头形状所指方向，将同行或同列的目标色块沿箭头方向平移，平移距离等于箭头自身的像素数量。\n- `Input structure`：黑色背景上有若干由同一颜色（如 red）绘制的箭头和若干由另一颜色（如 blue）绘制的小型实心矩形色块。每个箭头由一条直线段（水平或垂直，长度 2-4 像素）加上末端的一个三角尖端（由 1-2 个像素在垂直于线段的方向上构成）组成，箭头的尖端指示方向（上、下、左、右之一）。每个箭头与恰好一个 blue 色块处于同一行（若箭头水平）或同一列（若箭头垂直），且箭头位于色块的某一侧。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 识别每个箭头的朝向（由尖端位置确定：尖端在线段上方则指向上，尖端在线段右方则指向右，依此类推）。\n  - 统计该箭头由多少个 red 像素组成（包含线段和尖端），记为 N。\n  - 将与该箭头配对的 blue 色块沿箭头指向方向平移 N 个像素。\n  - 输出中箭头自身保持不变，色块在原位置处被清除为黑色背景。\n- `Key insight`：箭头同时编码了两层信息——方向和距离。方向由形状视觉直觉即可判断，但距离隐含在箭头的像素总数中，需要解题者将\"箭头大小\"与\"移动步数\"关联起来，而非仅看方向。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/31f7f899, train/1efba499, eval/247ef758, train/d93c6891, train/84551f4c, train/a1aa0c1e, train/e734a0e8, eval/332f06d7\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"31f7f899\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3862\nmechanism=0.5134\nlexical=0.0140\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, operation:count, operation:translate, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/31f7f899.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/31f7f899.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort vertical bars by height and redistribute them across fixed colored slots.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; a horizontal base line of a single color spans the full grid width.\n- Several monochrome vertical bars of distinct colors rise upward from the base line.\n\nRule:\n- Rank bar shapes by height (shortest to tallest).\n- Rank original x-positions (slots) left to right.\n- Place the i-th shortest shape into the i-th leftmost slot, recoloring it to that slot's original color.\n- The base line is preserved unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Each slot keeps its x-position and color identity; only the bar silhouettes are redistributed among slots by height order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"vertical bar sprite s\",\n  \"dst\": \"replace vertical bar d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.size.order == d.pos.col.order\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.color -> d.color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1efba499\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3426\nmechanism=0.4411\nlexical=0.0134\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:line_segment, object:marker, operation:bfs_distance, operation:translate, output:same_size, relation:aligned, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/1efba499.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/1efba499.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSwap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.\n- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.\n- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.\n- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"single marker pixels that have exactly one marker on the opposite side of the same row or column through the largest foreground-color barrier\",\n  \"path\": \"move along that row or column across the contiguous barrier segment\",\n  \"dst\": \"the background cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the barrier segment\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint only the final adjacent cell on each side with the opposite marker color\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase the original paired marker cells; leave the barrier and unpaired markers unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3580\nmechanism=0.4719\nlexical=0.0139\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/247ef758.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s in the left area whose color appears on the right frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"center at positions d in the right frame\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.same_row_frame_edges.color and s.color == d.same_col_frame_edges.color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"move sprites from bottom to top, with new covering old\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3773\nmechanism=0.4626\nlexical=0.0136\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate, output:same_size, relation:aligned, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d93c6891.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/d93c6891.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"gray one-cell-thick bars s\",\n  \"dst\": \"4-adjacent solid orange rectangles d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s 4-touches d\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase s to yellow; in each matched d, repaint |matched s cells| orange cells gray as complete rows for horizontal bars or complete columns for vertical bars from the touched edge\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3264\nmechanism=0.4240\nlexical=0.0192\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:marker, operation:count, output:same_size, relation:aligned, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/84551f4c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/84551f4c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\nRule\n- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\nKey insight\n- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars on the background; blue bars start chains and red bars may follow\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each blue-started chain, rotate each reached vertical bar clockwise onto the shared baseline; the next red bar is reached when its column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a1aa0c1e\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3432\nmechanism=0.4716\nlexical=0.0126\ncode_feature=0.1667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:line_segment, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, operation:count, operation:translate, relation:aligned, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/a1aa0c1e.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a1aa0c1e.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCompress the stacked colored bars into a compact ordered table with the control pixel on the selected row.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid contains several full-width colored bars from top to\n  bottom; the last bar is the base/separator color.\n- Each non-base bar may have an aligned three-column comb motif below it,\n  alternating side-pair rows with solid rows.\n- A singleton control pixel sits away from the colored motifs.\n\nRule\n- Make one output row per non-base bar, preserving top-to-bottom order.\n- In that row, paint the bar color once for each solid three-pixel row in\n  its motif, then paint the base-bar color in the separator column.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
